## Proof

**Setup and formulas.**

Since $f$ is continuous and strictly increasing on $[0,1]$ with $f\ge 0$, and $f$ is not identically zero (a strictly increasing function on $[0,1]$ with values in $[0,\infty)$ has $f(1)>f(0)\ge 0$, so $f(1)>0$), the region $R$ and the solid of revolution both have positive area/volume.

The $x$-coordinate of the centroid of $R$ is
$$x_1=\frac{\displaystyle\int_0^1 x\,f(x)\,dx}{\displaystyle\int_0^1 f(x)\,dx}.$$

Rotating $R$ about the $x$-axis, the cross-section at $x$ is a disk of radius $f(x)$, hence area $\pi f(x)^2$. Thus the $x$-coordinate of the centroid of the solid is
$$x_2=\frac{\displaystyle\int_0^1 x\,\pi f(x)^2\,dx}{\displaystyle\int_0^1 \pi f(x)^2\,dx}=\frac{\displaystyle\int_0^1 x\,f(x)^2\,dx}{\displaystyle\int_0^1 f(x)^2\,dx}.$$

**Reduction to a comparison of expectations.**

Define two probability densities on $[0,1]$:
$$w(x)=\frac{f(x)}{A},\qquad v(x)=\frac{f(x)^2}{B},\qquad A=\int_0^1 f(t)\,dt,\quad B=\int_0^1 f(t)^2\,dt.$$
(Both $A,B>0$ since $f$ is continuous, nonnegative, and $f(1)>0$.) Then $x_1=\mathbb{E}_w[x]$ and $x_2=\mathbb{E}_v[x]$, so we must show $\mathbb{E}_v[x]>\mathbb{E}_w[x]$.

**Relating the two measures.** The ratio
$$h(x):=\frac{v(x)}{w(x)}=\frac{f(x)^2/B}{f(x)/A}=\frac{A}{B}\,f(x)=c\,f(x),\qquad c:=\frac{A}{B}>0,$$
is, up to the positive constant $c$, just $f(x)$. Since $f$ is strictly increasing, **$h$ is strictly increasing**. Moreover
$$\mathbb{E}_w[h]=\int_0^1 h(x)\,w(x)\,dx=\int_0^1 c\,f(x)\cdot\frac{f(x)}{A}\,dx=\frac{c}{A}\int_0^1 f(x)^2\,dx=\frac{A/B}{A}\cdot B=1.$$

**Covariance identity.** Writing the $v$-expectation in terms of $w$:
$$\mathbb{E}_v[x]=\int_0^1 x\,v(x)\,dx=\int_0^1 x\,h(x)\,w(x)\,dx=\mathbb{E}_w[x\,h(x)]=\mathbb{E}_w[x]\,\mathbb{E}_w[h]+\operatorname{Cov}_w(x,h).$$
Since $\mathbb{E}_w[h]=1$, this gives
$$x_2=\mathbb{E}_v[x]=\mathbb{E}_w[x]+\operatorname{Cov}_w(x,h)=x_1+\operatorname{Cov}_w(x,h).$$

**Strict positivity of the covariance.** Using the standard identity
$$\operatorname{Cov}_w(x,h)=\frac12\int_0^1\!\!\int_0^1 (x-y)\bigl(h(x)-h(y)\bigr)\,w(x)\,w(y)\,dx\,dy.$$
Because $h$ is strictly increasing, for every $(x,y)$ with $x\ne y$ the two factors $(x-y)$ and $(h(x)-h(y))$ have the same sign, so the integrand is $>0$. The set $\{(x,y):x\ne y\}$ has full measure with respect to the product density $w(x)w(y)$ (the diagonal has measure $0$, and $w$ is a genuine density with $w>0$ on a set of positive measure). Hence the double integral is strictly positive:
$$\operatorname{Cov}_w(x,h)>0.$$

**Conclusion.** Therefore
$$x_2=x_1+\operatorname{Cov}_w(x,h)>x_1,$$
i.e. $\boxed{x_1<x_2}$, as required. $\blacksquare$

